    "body": "## What was broken\n\n`create_operator` declared `direction: str = \"bidirectional\"`. A string default is never `None`, so the branch meant to apply the CYCLE-25 per-op_type canonicalization \u2014\n\n```python\nif direction is None:\n    direction = self._canonical_direction(op_type, None)\n```\n\n\u2014 was **unreachable on the default path**. Ontology v3.6 \u00a75.2.7 says `NAND -> \"unidirectional\"`, so every caller that omitted `direction` got the opposite of the design: a **mutual** NAND.\n\n**Strong-consistency evidence for the shape:** the codebase already uses `direction: str | None = None` two functions away \u2014 `create_direct_edge` and `_find_operator`. The buggy site was the odd one out.\n\n**Why it matters (not cosmetic).** A bidirectional NAND damages *both* endpoints; a unidirectional one spares the attacker. Measured on the storage-architecture decision, the most discriminating criterion accumulated `beta=19.16` and fell to **confidence 0.113** \u2014 the lowest of ten \u2014 inverting the ranking so the best-measured option ranked **9th of 12**. Re-wired unidirectional it ranked **first**.\n\n## The fix\n\n**Two sites \u2014 because fixing only the SDK leaves the fix inert for every agent.**\n\n| file | before | after |\n|---|---|---|\n| `tortoise/sdk.py:10117` | `direction: str = \"bidirectional\"` | `direction: str \\| None = None` |\n| `tortoise/mcp_server.py:2067` | `direction: str = \"bidirectional\"` | `direction: str \\| None = None` |\n\n`mcp_server.py` carried the **same defect**, and it is the surface agents actually call: it passed `direction` through **explicitly**, so a non-`None` value reached `create_operator` and the canonicalization never ran there either. Fixing only `sdk.py` would have left every agent-created NAND mutual \u2014 the fix would have been untestable in production.\n\nBoth docstrings are reconciled into **one** statement of the default. They previously contradicted each other \u2014 the parameter docstring said *\"Default bidirectional (mutual) for all op types\"* while `_canonical_direction`'s said NAND \u2192 unidirectional \u2014 and that contradiction is how this survived.\n\n## Blast radius \u2014 measured, not assumed\n\nThe issue asked me to check whether the extraction path already passes `direction` explicitly. **It does, so ingest is NOT affected.**\n\n- **Unaffected:** the batch/ingest path. `sdk.py:12313` does `direction = conn.get(\"direction\")` and `:12350` passes it through, so an absent key already became `None` and **already canonicalized correctly**. `commit_ops.py` passes an explicit value, so it is unaffected too.\n- **Affected \u2014 direct API callers that omit the argument.** Measured call sites: `mining.py` (draft-to-draft refute wiring), `__main__.py` (CLI truth/relevance edges, NAND by default), `sdk.py:9577` (`promote_point` temporal NAND), and the `graph-scripts/decide*.py` paths.\n\n**Existing NAND edges keep their stored `direction`.** This changes new writes only, so a graph written before and after holds both semantics. No migration is needed or included.\n\n## Tests\n\nTests that asserted the bugged default were **updated, not cited** \u2014 they *encoded* the bug:\n\n- `test_nand_defaults_to_bidirectional` \u2192 **`test_operator_direction_defaults_are_per_op_type`**, one regression test asserting all three acceptance legs, so the shadowing cannot silently return:\n  - `create_operator(\"NAND\", a, [b])` \u2192 `\"unidirectional\"`\n  - `create_operator(\"NAND\", a, [b], direction=\"bidirectional\")` \u2192 `\"bidirectional\"`\n  - `create_operator(\"IMPL\", a, [b])` \u2192 `\"bidirectional\"`\n- The MCP-surface leg (`test_mcp_tool_honors_direction`) updated to assert `\"unidirectional\"` on the default path \u2014 this is the leg that proves the `mcp_server.py` half.\n- Module docstring reconciled.\n\n## Verification\n\n**759 passing across 6 suites:** `test_ep_directed_nand` 7, `tests/eval/why_suite` 68, EP set 85, mining/dedup/sdk/decide 189, ranking/recall/calibration set 190, remaining NAND-touching set 220.\n\nOne observed failure is reported rather than hidden: `test_ep_local_395::test_ac5_interactive_latency_zone` (local EP took 3.38 s against a 1.2 s interactive budget). **It cannot be reached by this change** \u2014 it builds its graph through `_chain(...)`, which passes `op_type=\"IMPL\"` and `direction=\"bidirectional\"` explicitly, so the direction-absent path is never taken. It is a wall-clock assertion under host load (load average **230.80** on 10 CPUs at the time). Raised here so a reviewer can judge it rather than discover it.\n\n**Not changed, deliberately:** `commit_ops.py`'s `direction=_op_attr(op, \"direction\") or \"unidirectional\"` fallback forces unidirectional for *every* op_type, including IMPL, which contradicts the batch path's IMPL\u2192bidirectional. That is a separate latent defect with its own blast radius, not the shadowing this issue directs me to fix; recorded on #7813 rather than folded in.\n\nCloses #7813\n\nreview recorded: reviews/7820.json verdict=clean @ c33f3d9e5e23f7e3ce53ed8978b1b5e4ec64308d diff=8b6d4eb3ca59ac5e2d2b37e9d942fd30ac931b3ed011d05318c330a31f282350 (daniel-ospina/tortoise) sig=e56ad9123d00c55ab0bfa233274f3906e7323f666dccc0c78eb4db05aa44c30f",
